\title{Meta-analysis of treatment effect modification from modifier-level aggregate data}
\description{
\strong{interactmeta} implements \strong{contribution-adjusted meta-analysis
(CAMS)} for treatment effect modification from
modifier-level aggregate data (Panaro, Röver & Friede 2025). Each trial
contributes two modifier-level estimates; CAMS derives an information fraction
\eqn{\pi_j}, fits a random-effects meta-analysis of within-trial
contrasts and a random-effects meta-regression of precision-weighted
combined effects, then reports the interaction \eqn{\gamma}, modifier-level
means \eqn{\mu_A(\pi)} and \eqn{\mu_B(\pi)}, and the combined-population
mean \eqn{M(\pi)} at a chosen reporting fraction.
}
\details{
Fitter: \code{\link[=cams]{cams()}}. Bayesian inference (default) uses \strong{bayesmeta};
frequentist inference uses \strong{metafor} (REML or ML). The statistical
model is the same; intervals are credible or confidence intervals
respectively. See \code{vignette("cams")}.

\code{\link[=da]{da()}} (Difference of Averages) and \code{\link[=ad]{ad()}} (Average Difference) are
\strong{different constructions} (Panaro, Röver & Friede 2026), not two names
for one estimand and not alternative CAMS models. \code{\link[=swada]{swada()}} applies the
Same Weighting Across Different Analyses (SWADA) principle so those
constructions share study contributions.
\code{\link[=conventional]{conventional()}} is a compatibility bundle of DA and AD with
analysis-specific weights. A Bayesian posterior on DA or AD does not add
information-fraction estimands.
}
\section{CAMS workflow}{

\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=ifrac]{ifrac()}} (optional before fit) builds trial information fractions
\eqn{\pi_j=\sigma_{Bj}^{-2}/(\sigma_{Aj}^{-2}+\sigma_{Bj}^{-2})};
\code{\link[=cams]{cams()}} computes them from \code{vi} by default.
\item Within each trial, form contrast \eqn{g_j=y_{Bj}-y_{Aj}} and combined
coordinate \eqn{m_j=(1-\pi_j)y_{Aj}+\pi_j y_{Bj}}.
\item Fit a random-effects meta-analysis of \eqn{g_j} and a random-effects
meta-regression of \eqn{m_j} on \eqn{\pi_j}.
\item \code{\link[=interactions]{interactions()}}, \code{\link[=modifiers]{modifiers()}}, and \code{\link[=combined]{combined()}} summarize
\eqn{\gamma} and effects at the reporting information fraction.
\item \code{\link[=plot]{plot()}} and \code{\link[=forestplot]{forestplot()}} visualize effects across \eqn{\pi} and by
trial (see \code{vignette("cams")} and \code{vignette("worked-examples")}).
}
}

