\section{Inference}{


CAMS can be fitted using Bayesian or frequentist inference. These are
not two different scientific models. Default Bayesian fitting uses
\strong{bayesmeta} (Röver 2020); pass \code{method = "REML"} (or \code{"ML"}) for
\strong{metafor} (Viechtbauer 2010). See \code{vignette("cams")}.

Default Bayesian heterogeneity priors match \code{\link[bayesmeta:bayesmeta]{bayesmeta::bayesmeta()}}:
improper uniform on \eqn{\tau_m} and \eqn{\tau_g}. Fixed effects use
independent \eqn{\mathrm{Normal}(0,10)} on \eqn{(\alpha,\theta)} and on
\eqn{\gamma}.
Empirical work indicates interaction heterogeneity is often smaller than
overall heterogeneity (Panaro, Röver & Friede, arXiv:2606.23968); vignettes
set half-normal scales from that literature explicitly. See \code{\link[=cams]{cams()}}.
}

\section{Reporting}{


\code{\link[=plot]{plot()}} (default contribution view) plots modifier-level and combined effects
across the information fraction. \code{\link[=forestplot]{forestplot()}} shows observed estimates and
trial-level CAMS shrinkage (grey markers; \code{include_shrinkage = TRUE} by
default). Typography helpers
(\code{\link[=interactmeta_theme_report]{interactmeta_theme_report()}}, \code{\link[=interactmeta_palette]{interactmeta_palette()}}) are documented
on their help pages.
}

